We probe whether counterfactual labels can train a step-level router, as a diagnostic of label informativeness. A classification head on LLM hidden states degenerates to always-deterministic across all configurations. An implicit router (Qwen3-1.7B fine-tuned; Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}) yields $54.1 \pm 5.4\%$ step-level balanced accuracy but $47.1 \pm 2.2\%$ on held-out episodes (8 seeds)---below always-deterministic (50\%). This reveals a capacity-dependent negative: at practical router scales (0.6B--1.7B), episode-level balanced accuracy remains at or below chance ($50.67\%$/$47.1\%$). Scaling to 4B yields $58.96 \pm 1.94\%$ ($p < 0.005$ vs.\ random; Appendix~\ref{app:ablations})---confirming that episode-level signal exists but is capacity-starved. Augmentation-based data scaling ($25\%$--$800\%$) shows no improvement at any capacity (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}); however, paraphrase augmentation inflates the majority class (89.6\%$\rightarrow$97.7\%) without generating novel routing patterns. Step-level signal scarcity is ruled out (majority-class paraphrasing saturates), but episode-level minority-class diversity cannot be assessed without additional real data collection---an estimated ${\sim}1{,}350$ episode pairs for 50 minority-class examples at current dynamics. Capacity scaling results are non-monotonic (0.6B: 50.7\%, 1.7B: 47.1\%, 4B: 59.0\%), with only 4B exceeding the majority-vote baseline; configuration differences (8 vs.\ 4 seeds for 1.7B vs.\ others) may contribute to non-monotonicity. We interpret this as preliminary evidence that larger capacity helps, pending controlled-configuration confirmation. Notably, scaling model capacity improves episode-level routing ($+9$\,pp at 4B) without improving step-level accuracy (flat ${\sim}53\%$ across scales), confirming that the extractable signal is task-type-level structure---consistent with \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}'s finding that death risk (a task-level property) dominates routing utility