\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Cross-environment evidence.}
On ALFWorld ($N{=}135$), the framework identifies a qualitatively different mechanism: information asymmetry (access to admissible actions, reversible navigation failures) rather than death avoidance (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-env}). Neither MATH nor ALFWorld validates DAF quantitatively---true replication requires a second catastrophic-failure environment (e.g., NetHack, Crafter). These cross-domain contrasts demonstrate framework applicability, not universality of the death-dominance finding.

\paragraph{Penalty sensitivity and correlation structure.}
The $r = 0.97$ correlation is specific to catastrophic death ($P{=}100$); defining failure as any non-positive score yields $r = -0.13$ ($p = 0.70$). As shown in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}, this correlation is largely a mathematical consequence of death dominance (partial $r = 0.02$). Death is effectively the only component generating differential routing signal; the 42.9\% internal death rate reflects 7B-scale hallucination vulnerability, and larger models with lower death rates would reduce DAF.

